 India y los Balcanes","book":"Historia del jazz","author":"Ted Gioia","start":1959,"end":2000,"desc":"Para Gioia, la tendencia más ignorada de la historia del jazz: su conversión en parte de la cultura musical mundial. Ivo Papasov funde Parker y Goodman con las bodas balcánicas; Biréli Lagrène prolonga a Django; Hamish Moore toca jazz con gaita; L. Subramaniam, L. Shankar y Shakti exploran la India; Trilok Gurtu lleva la tabla al jazz; Jan Garbarek adapta la canción noruega. Brasil va más allá de la bossa nova con Hermeto Pascoal («un genio», según Airto), Egberto Gismonti, Airto Moreira, Flora Purim y la voz de Milton Nascimento (Clube da Esquina). En Sudáfrica, los Jazz Epistles (1959) lanzan a Abdullah Ibrahim, protegido de Ellington, y a Hugh Masekela; Chris McGregor sigue en Londres. Los cubanos Paquito D'Rivera, Arturo Sandoval y Gonzalo Rubalcaba, los europeos en Nueva York (Petrucciani, Makowicz, Toots Thielemans), el free europeo (Brötzmann, Schlippenbach, Evan Parker, Derek Bailey) y Charlie Haden con la Guerra Civil española completan el mapa.","color":"#a3e635","family":"Fusión: jazz-rock, ECM y músicas del mundo","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/jazz/#fusion-mundial"}],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Festival Co-Creator Role","body":"Hugh Masekela, an exiled jazz musician from South Africa, co-conceived the Zaire 74 festival with Stewart Levine. They had met about 10 years prior at the Manhattan School of Music and developed a lifelong friendship, envisioning the festival as an opportunity to unite African American and African performers. \"The idea for the festival came from Stewart Levine, a native New Yorker, and the exiled from South Africa jazz musician Hugh Massakella.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 33 - Zaire '74, Muhammad Ali, and The Rumble in the Jungle","url":null}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":105337512336289,"type":"alb